dataptrs_textptr_i386.exe -- i386 PE whose .text holds relocated instruction
operands and, right after an instruction naming the same function, a pointer
table.

What it is
  A 2,048-byte PE32 (SHA-256
  e4f447d1ba243b22b96f12a22c55c9dcec5476effb338aab3320e5b9034fce66) assembled
  from dataptrs_textptr.s. Its .reloc lists, in .text:
    0x401001  push $_helper            operand -> _helper   (0x40104e)
    0x40101f  jmp *cases(,%eax,4)      operand -> the case table
    0x401023.. the four case words     -> the case bodies
    0x40104f  mov $_dispatch, %eax     operand -> _dispatch (0x401013)
    0x401054  _text_table[0]           -> _helper
    0x401058  _text_table[1]           -> _dispatch
  and in .data _data_table (0x403000, 0x403004) -> _helper, _dispatch.
  _text_table[1] starts five bytes after the mov that loads _dispatch's
  address: inside the byte window an operand could occupy, outside the mov.

How it was built (host clang + the rustup-bundled lld-link)
  clang -target i686-pc-windows-msvc -c dataptrs_textptr.s -o t.obj
  rust-lld -flavor link /brepro /dynamicbase /safeseh:no /entry:start \
           /subsystem:console /nodefaultlib /out:dataptrs_textptr_i386.exe t.obj
  Ubuntu clang 14.0.0-1ubuntu1.1, LLD 20.1.8 (rustc 1.90.0). Prebuilt because CI
  has no PE linker.

What it pins
  `kuna xrefs --to 0x401013` lists _text_table[1] and _data_table[1] as pointer
  rows, not the mov's operand; `--to 0x40104e` lists _text_table[0] and
  _data_table[0], not the push's operand.
